\description{
Combines a non-probability sample with a probability reference sample to
produce population-representative weights. The procedure is:
}
\details{
\enumerate{
\item Fit the \strong{nested propensity score} (NPS) model on the starting
model \code{start}, giving base weights and a baseline log-likelihood.
\item \strong{Greedy variable selection} (GVS): a forward likelihood-ratio
search over the terms in \code{scope} but not in \code{start} (via \code{\link[=gvs_step]{gvs_step()}}) at
significance level \code{alpha}, adding whichever significant candidate has the
largest average increment in the log pseudo-likelihood.
\item \strong{LIFO stepwise raking}: rake the full selected model to the
population margins. If raking does not converge, pop the last-added term,
refit NPS, and rake again -- last in, first out -- until it converges or
no selected term is left.
}

Those three stages are what the method is named for in Chen et al. (2025):
\strong{R}aking with \strong{A}ssisted Nested Propens\strong{i}ty Score and
\strong{L}IFO \strong{S}election.

All computation runs on aggregated covariate cells, so cost scales with the
number of distinct covariate patterns rather than the sample size.
}
\section{Benchmark names}{

With \code{benchmarks = TRUE}, \code{fit$benchmarks} carries the comparison methods.
They correspond to the estimators tabulated in Chen et al. (2025):\tabular{lll}{
   package \tab paper \tab description \cr
   \code{d_unweighted} \tab \code{naive} \tab the unweighted cohort mean \cr
   \code{d_cal1} \tab \code{cal-1} \tab raking to univariate margins from equal weights \cr
   \code{d_cal2} \tab \code{cal-2} \tab raking on main effects and all two-way terms \cr
   \code{d_nps1} \tab \code{nps-1} \tab NPS weights, main effects only \cr
   \code{d_nps2} \tab \code{nps-2} \tab NPS weights through the starting model \cr
   \code{d_nps1_rake} \tab \code{nps-cal-1} \tab NPS main effects, then univariate raking \cr
   \code{d_nps2_rake} \tab  \tab NPS through the starting model, then raking \cr
}


\code{cal-2} is the one the paper reports as failing to converge in over 99 percent
of simulations; here it returns \code{NA} with a warning when raking fails, so a
non-convergent benchmark is visible rather than silently absent.
}

\section{Reproducing the published estimator}{

The defaults -- \code{start = 2}, \code{scope = 3}, \code{fallback = FALSE} -- are exactly
the procedure of \code{fun.rails.threeway()}, so \code{rails()} and the deprecated shim
give identical weights on identical input. Changing \code{start} or \code{scope} departs
from the paper deliberately; \code{fallback = TRUE} departs from it only in the
degenerate case where the published code would have returned \code{NA}.
}

\section{Aggregated input}{

\code{aggregated = TRUE} accepts cell tables directly, which is what the original
\code{fun.rails.threeway()} required. It is the right choice when the microdata
cannot be materialized in one frame. The cost is that the fit no longer knows
which record belongs to which cell, so per-record weights and the variance
estimator are unavailable.
}

\examples{
pop <- rails_simulate(20000, seed = 42)
aou <- pop[pop$aou == 1, ]
ref <- pop[pop$s == 1, ]
ref$dweight <- 1 / ref$ps

fit <- rails(aou, ref, vars = c("agegroup", "sex", "income"),
             weights_ref = "dweight", verbose = FALSE)
fit

# Weighted prevalence of the outcome, against the population truth:
w <- weights(fit)
sum(w * aou$y) / sum(w)
mean(pop$y)

}
